      "why": "unrolled_k with k=2-4 can achieve higher throughput than iterative (1 result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles) while keeping area close to iterative (shared datapath, no pipeline registers, just k parallel shift/add stages). For N=8-12 and k=2-4, latency is 4-9 cycles, so throughput = Fmax/latency. If Fmax is ~100-200 MHz, throughput can reach 1-25 MSPS. This family may offer a better area-throughput trade-off than iterative for meeting the 1 MSPS constraint with lower area, since it can use fewer iterations (lower N) to meet accuracy while k boosts throughput. I limit k to 2-4 to keep area reasonable (k=8 would roughly double the datapath area)."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires >=1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area as the primary objective. Among my assigned families (iterative, unrolled_k), both are low-area (no pipeline registers). The key trade-off is: iterative has the smallest area but lowest throughput (1 result per N+3 cycles), while unrolled_k trades some area (k parallel stages) for higher throughput (1 result per ceil(N/k)+3 cycles). To meet 1 MSPS, I need Fmax/latency >= 1 MHz. For iterative with N=10, latency=13, so Fmax >= 13 MHz is needed (easy). For unrolled_k with N=10, k=2, latency=8, so Fmax >= 8 MHz. Both are feasible. The error constraint requires sufficient n_iter and data_width. I focus data_width on 10-16 (below 10, output LSB is too coarse to hit 2^-10 error; above 16, area grows with diminishing accuracy returns). n_iter 4-16 covers the accuracy range. angle_guard -2 to 2 and frac_guard 0-2 cover the accuracy-relevant guard bits without excessive width. I split the budget 50/50 between the two families to map the Pareto front across both, since unrolled_k may dominate iterative on the throughput-area trade-off for this spec."
}

